WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.035 --> 00:00:21.200
Captain Nemo and I returned to the gallery, where we settled ourselves on one of the couches. He showed me a set of drawings of the Nautilus.

2
00:00:21.202 --> 00:00:32.431
"As you can see, Professor Aronnax," said the captain, "my ship is shaped like a cigar, measuring 232 feet in length and 26 feet at its widest point.

3
00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:39.352
It has two steel hulls joined together, which make it as strong as if it were made of solid metal."

4
00:00:39.353 --> 00:00:43.669
"How does it sink under the water?" I asked, studying the plans.

5
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:49.661
"The submarine has reservoirs that can be filled with seawater when we need to descend," he explained.

6
00:00:49.662 --> 00:00:55.645
"But we don't have to take on a huge amount of water because everything has a natural tendency to sink in water."

7
00:00:55.646 --> 00:00:59.582
"Then how do you make the submarine rise to the surface?"

8
00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:03.589
"All we have to do is pump out the extra water," said the captain.

9
00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:07.335
"The pumps, which run on electricity, are very powerful.

10
00:01:07.336 --> 00:01:12.161
But I'm sure you observed that when jets of water burst over the Abraham Lincoln."

11
00:01:12.162 --> 00:01:14.407
I remembered that quite well.

12
00:01:14.408 --> 00:01:20.812
"The submarine also has rudders on either side that help to guide it up or down," the captain went on.

13
00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:24.447
"But how can the pilot see where he's going?" I asked.

14
00:01:24.448 --> 00:01:31.539
"He sits in a glass pilot house on top of the vessel. Behind him is a powerful electric light that shows him the way."

15
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:41.717
"Aha!" I said. "That explains the glow of the 'narwhal' that puzzled us so. Well, Captain, your Nautilus is certainly a marvelous vessel."

16
00:01:41.718 --> 00:01:46.443
"It is indeed, Professor, and I love it as if it were a part of myself.

17
00:01:46.444 --> 00:01:51.526
I am the engineer, builder, and captain of this wonderful boat."

18
00:01:51.527 --> 00:01:55.526
"But how did you manage to build it in secret?" I asked.

19
00:01:55.527 --> 00:01:59.368
"Each part came from a different place," said Captain Nemo.

20
00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:06.772
"The keel was made in France, the propeller in Scotland, the engine in Prussia, and the instruments in New York.

21
00:02:06.773 --> 00:02:12.605
I gave a different name to every supplier, so that no one could trace the orders back to me."

22
00:02:12.606 --> 00:02:18.764
"Yes, but someone had to put the various parts together," I said, still mystified.

23
00:02:18.765 --> 00:02:27.438
"I set up my workshop on a desert island. There my crew, whom I had trained, put the Nautilus together under my direction.

24
00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:30.764
When we finished, we burned all traces of our work."

25
00:02:30.765 --> 00:02:37.486
I couldn't begin to imagine how much this vessel was worth, especially considering the value of the treasures onboard.

26
00:02:37.487 --> 00:02:42.335
"One last question, Captain Nemo," I said. "Are you rich?"

27
00:02:42.336 --> 00:02:48.510
"Immensely rich, Professor. I could pay the national debt of France and never miss the money."

28
00:02:48.511 --> 00:02:53.740
I stared at him. Was he pulling my leg? Time would tell.

29
00:02:53.741 --> 00:02:56.999
"Now," said Captain Nemo, "it's almost noon.

30
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.970
Time for us to figure out our location and determine our starting point for this voyage. We need to go back up to the surface."

31
00:03:03.971 --> 00:03:06.899
The captain pressed a button three times.

32
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:14.057
The pumps began to drive the water from the tanks; the needle on one of his instruments showed that we were rising to the surface.

33
00:03:14.058 --> 00:03:16.058
The needle stopped moving.

34
00:03:16.059 --> 00:03:22.136
"We've arrived," said the captain, who then led the way to a platform on top of the submarine.

35
00:03:22.137 --> 00:03:25.382
The platform was only three feet above the water.

36
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:31.359
Toward the middle of the platform I saw the slight bump that indicated where the small boat was stored.

37
00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:41.708
I also observed the two glass enclosures that the captain had described, one for the pilot and the other for the electric light that guided him.

38
00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:49.772
The sea was beautiful and the sky was clear. A light breeze rippled the surface of the water. There was nothing in sight.

39
00:03:49.773 --> 00:03:56.097
Captain Nemo used his sextant to measure the altitude of the sun, thus determining our latitude.

40
00:03:56.098 --> 00:04:04.184
"It's noon," said the captain. "Professor, whenever you're ready . . ." He indicated the hatch that led to the submarine's interior.

41
00:04:04.185 --> 00:04:10.914
I took one last look at the sea and climbed down the ladder. I felt the vessel beginning to descend.

42
00:04:10.915 --> 00:04:18.930
In the gallery Captain Nemo used another instrument to calculate our longitude and determine our exact position on the sea.

43
00:04:18.931 --> 00:04:24.755
"Our voyage under the seas began at noon today, November 8," he said.

44
00:04:24.756 --> 00:04:33.247
"We are headed northeast at a depth of 156 feet. Here are some maps upon which you can follow our course." He bowed to me.

45
00:04:33.248 --> 00:04:36.311
"And now I'll leave you to your studies."

46
00:04:36.312 --> 00:04:41.478
I spent the next hour thinking about Captain Nemo and his amazing submarine.

47
00:04:41.479 --> 00:04:47.399
Then I noticed a large map on the table, and I put my finger on the spot where our voyage had begun.

48
00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:53.232
I realized that we were following the Black River, a powerful ocean current like the Gulf Stream.

49
00:04:53.233 --> 00:04:58.970
I was still staring at the map when Ned and Conseil appeared at the door of the gallery.

50
00:04:58.971 --> 00:05:03.876
They were standing still, frozen at the sight of the wonders spread before them.

51
00:05:03.877 --> 00:05:09.026
"Where are we?" cried Ned. "In a museum in Quebec?"

52
00:05:09.027 --> 00:05:13.804
"No, it must be a museum in Paris," insisted Conseil.

53
00:05:13.805 --> 00:05:25.288
I motioned for them to enter. "My friends, you are in neither Canada nor France but onboard the Nautilus, 156 feet below sea level."

54
00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:33.598
"Professor Aronnax," said Ned, "can you tell me how many men are onboard with us? Ten, twenty, fifty, a hundred?"

55
00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:41.788
"I don't know," I replied. "I think you should give up any idea of seizing the Nautilus or escaping from it right now.

56
00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:50.423
This ship is a masterpiece of modern industry, and I'm glad I have the chance to see it. So be quiet for a while and let's see what happens."

57
00:05:50.424 --> 00:05:57.344
"See!" exclaimed Ned. "But we can see nothing in this underwater prison! We're sailing blindly!"

58
00:05:57.345 --> 00:06:01.756
Ned had barely said these words when all the lights went out.

59
00:06:01.757 --> 00:06:06.002
The three of us didn't dare to speak; we didn't know what would happen next.

60
00:06:06.003 --> 00:06:09.502
We heard something that sounded like panels sliding.

61
00:06:09.503 --> 00:06:12.002
"It's the end!" cried Ned.

62
00:06:12.003 --> 00:06:17.907
Suddenly light appeared at either end of the gallery, thanks to two large windows.

63
00:06:17.908 --> 00:06:24.716
The sea was clearly visible for a mile around the Nautilus. What a spectacle!

64
00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:30.297
"Well, you wished to see, Ned," I said. "Now you can see."

65
00:06:30.298 --> 00:06:36.725
"Amazing! Truly amazing!" muttered the harpooner, forgetting his bad mood.

66
00:06:36.726 --> 00:06:40.637
Conseil was equally captivated by the view through the windows.

67
00:06:40.638 --> 00:06:46.042
As Ned named the fish, Conseil put them into their scientific classifications.

68
00:06:46.043 --> 00:06:56.051
I won't mention all the varieties that passed before my dazzled eyes for two hours, but they represented a superb collection of specimens that I had only read about.

69
00:06:56.052 --> 00:07:04.448
These fish, more numerous than the birds of the air, were undoubtedly attracted by the brilliance of the submarine's electric light.

70
00:07:04.449 --> 00:07:10.870
Suddenly there was light in the gallery, the panels closed, and the enchanting vision disappeared.

71
00:07:10.871 --> 00:07:14.958
I expected Captain Nemo to join us, but he didn't appear.

72
00:07:14.959 --> 00:07:21.687
Ned and Conseil returned to their cabin, and I returned to mine, where I found that my dinner was ready.

73
00:07:21.688 --> 00:07:25.926
I passed the evening reading, writing, and thinking.

74
00:07:25.927 --> 00:07:34.069
That night I slept deeply while the Nautilus glided rapidly through the current of the Black River.
